The candidate does not survive the joint G/H/V test. The paper’s exact finite predicate (“\(S\) is \(k\)-free mod \(b\)”) is cheaply checkable, but its generic witnesses are explicitly constructible; the only computationally difficult part studied is finding unusually high harmonic-sum sets. That is an optimization search, and the paper supplies only a few fixed record witnesses—not an answer-first mechanism for unlimited hard instances. I’m documenting the rejection instead of manufacturing a module whose hardness claim would be unsupported.
